-- allium: 3
-- agent-health.allium
--
-- Scope: Classification of tmux pane activity, crashed-agent detection,
--        notifications (needs-input, review), and Claude Code hook handlers.
-- Excludes: Task lifecycle (see tasks.allium); MCP tools (see mcp-task-tools.allium);
--           dispatch and retry (see dispatch.allium).

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    active_threshold: Duration = 10.minutes
    shell_stale_threshold: Duration = 4.hours
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Agent Health ==

rule ClassifyAgentActivity {
    -- Derives sub_status for running tasks from hook event timestamps.
    when: Tick()
    for task in core/Tasks where status = running and tmux_window != null and sub_status not in {crashed, conflict}:
        ensures:
            if task.last_notification_at != null and (task.last_pre_tool_use_at = null or task.last_notification_at > task.last_pre_tool_use_at):
                task.sub_status = needs_input
            else if task.live_subagents > 0:
                task.sub_status = active
            else if task.live_shells > 0:
                if task.oldest_live_shell_started_at != null and now - task.oldest_live_shell_started_at > config.shell_stale_threshold:
                    task.sub_status = stale_shell
                else:
                    task.sub_status = active
            else if task.last_pre_tool_use_at != null and now - task.last_pre_tool_use_at <= config.active_threshold:
                task.sub_status = active
            else:
                task.sub_status = stale

    @guidance
        -- config.active_threshold (default 10 minutes) is the window after
        -- the last PreToolUse hook before a task is considered Stale.
        -- Crashed and Conflict are orthogonal — set by DetectCrashedAgent
        -- and conflict detectors respectively, never overridden here.
        -- The wallclock Staleness enum (Fresh/Aging/Stale at 3d/7d) is
        -- independent — it decorates card ages.
        -- Every transition into running stamps last_pre_tool_use_at = now
        -- (DispatchTask on Backlog→Running, ResumeTask on resume, and the
        -- manual move handler on any transition into Running — Review→Running
        -- and Backlog→Running alike) so this classifier returns
        -- Active through the active_threshold window before the agent's first
        -- real PreToolUse hook fires. The manual-move seed is deliberately
        -- unconditional on the source status: a manual Backlog→Running move
        -- provisions no worktree, so the stamp has no observable effect there
        -- (both this classifier and DetectCrashedAgent only consider tasks
        -- with a tmux_window). Without seeding on the manual Review→
        -- Running path, a task whose Stop hook cleared the timestamp would
        -- be classified Stale on the very next tick — and because the card
        -- derives its minute label from the same timestamp, it would render
        -- the meaningless "stale · 0m". Recovery from Stale → Active is
        -- driven entirely by fresh PreToolUse hook timestamps (and
        -- Notification timestamps for needs_input); there is no separate
        -- in-memory activity timer.
        -- Ownership invariant: only hook events (PreToolUse / Stop), the
        -- "transition into Running" seeds, and the reverse of one of those
        -- seeds (the epic chain returning a claimed-but-unprovisioned subtask
        -- to Backlog clears the stamp its own claim wrote — see
        -- AutoDispatchNextSubtask in epics.allium) write
        -- last_pre_tool_use_at. Generic in-memory persists from the TUI
        -- (sort_order swaps, tick sub_status updates, editor saves) MUST NOT
        -- include this column — a stale in-memory snapshot would otherwise
        -- overwrite a fresher hook write and flick the task into Stale on the
        -- next tick.
        -- This is field ownership, not a database-only rule: it binds both
        -- directions of a persist. When such a persist writes a value the
        -- caller could not compute itself (the completion-recency sort_order
        -- the service derives on a done transition — see ConfirmDone in
        -- tasks.allium) and reflects it straight back into the in-memory
        -- board, it must patch that one field onto the board's current copy
        -- of the task, never re-impose the snapshot it submitted. Re-imposing
        -- the snapshot would carry its stale last_pre_tool_use_at along and
        -- produce the same Stale flick in memory that omitting the column
        -- from the write prevents in the database. Such a write-back is also
        -- a no-op for a task no longer on the board, so it cannot resurrect
        -- one that was deleted or archived while the write was in flight.
        -- Card label: when sub_status = stale, the card renders the idle
        -- time as "stale · Xm" where X = now - last_pre_tool_use_at. If
        -- last_pre_tool_use_at is null (legacy pre-v50 rows, or a transient
        -- window before the seed write commits), the card renders just
        -- "stale" with no minute suffix rather than "stale · 0m".
        -- Only tasks with a live tmux_window are classified (tasks without
        -- one have no running agent to observe).
        -- Live subagents outrank the staleness threshold but lose to
        -- needs_input: a permission prompt genuinely needs a human even
        -- while subagents churn, so hiding it behind a subagent count would
        -- trade one misreport for a worse one.
        -- That ordering is safe only because every notification that does
        -- NOT mean "a human is needed" is filtered out before it can stamp
        -- last_notification_at. auth_success is dropped outright; an
        -- idle_prompt is dropped conditionally, when the task's own shells or
        -- subagents are live. Both are decided upstream, at stamp time, by
        -- HookNotification.
        -- This rule reads timestamps and never the notification kind, so the
        -- distinction has to be made there or not at all -- see
        -- HookNotification's guidance for the full argument.
        -- live_shells > 0 sits below live_subagents (a genuinely live
        -- subagent always wins over an old-looking shell) but above the
        -- plain time-threshold branch, and is checked against its own,
        -- much longer config.shell_stale_threshold (4 hours) rather than
        -- config.active_threshold. This is deliberate: a legitimate dev
        -- server or long build can run for hours, so exempting live_shells
        -- from the 10-minute window is the whole point of this design (see
        -- docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-15-shell-visibility-design.md) --
        -- but exempting it *forever* would make a genuinely abandoned shell
        -- indistinguishable from a healthy one, so past
        -- shell_stale_threshold the sub-status becomes stale_shell instead
        -- of active, rendered on the card distinctly from plain stale
        -- ("shell stale · Xh" vs "stale · Xm") so an operator can tell which
        -- kind of inactivity they're looking at.
        -- Card label: an Active running task with live_subagents = 0 renders
        -- just "running". A positive count appends the subagent count:
        -- "running · 1 agent" (singular) or "running · N agents" for N > 1
        -- (plural), read straight from task.live_subagents.
}

rule DetectCrashedAgent {
    -- Fires when the tmux window disappears for a running task.
    when: WindowGone(task)

    requires: task.status = running

    ensures: task.sub_status = crashed
    ensures: task.tmux_window = null
    ensures:
        for entry in core/SubagentEntries where task = task:
            not exists entry
    ensures: task.live_subagents = 0
    ensures: task.stop_pending = false
    ensures:
        if notifications_enabled:
            DesktopNotification(
                task: task,
                urgency: urgent,
                about: agent_crashed
            )

    @guidance
        -- Clears every SubagentEntry for the task and resets live_subagents
        -- to 0, but does NOT run the HookSubagentStop drain path even if
        -- stop_pending was set. This rule already assigns sub_status =
        -- crashed; draining to Review in the same write would leave the
        -- task Crashed-and-in-Review. Crashed is the more informative
        -- state and wins. stop_pending is still cleared, so the bit cannot
        -- survive into the next session and fire a spurious flip later.
        -- Keeping the crash out of Review does not depend on that clear,
        -- nor on the order in which anything evaluates. A deferred Stop is
        -- applied only inside the write that drains the count to zero (see
        -- HookSubagentStop), and this rule's clear is the non-draining kind,
        -- which never assigns status. So there is no separate step that
        -- could observe a crashed task carrying the bit and flip it.
        -- Triggered by the WindowGone message, which is sent when the
        -- runtime detects that the task's tmux window no longer exists.
        -- The tmux_window reference is cleared by the same write that sets
        -- crashed: the window it named is gone by definition, and keeping the
        -- reference would only make the next tick re-check (and re-report) a
        -- window that cannot come back. Nothing downstream needs the name — the
        -- ConfirmRetry prompt labels itself from sub_status (crashed vs stale),
        -- and RetryResume kills an old window only when one is still referenced.
        -- Recovery from Crashed happens via RetryResume, which dispatches a
        -- fresh agent and resets sub_status to active.
        -- ClassifyAgentActivity intentionally skips Crashed and
        -- Conflict so the human-actionable state is not silently masked
        -- by stale hook timestamps.
        -- For non-running tasks (e.g. review tasks), window closure
        -- silently clears task.tmux_window = null without changing
        -- sub_status or sending notifications. This is the normal exit
        -- path for review agents and detached sessions.
        -- A tmux query failure (e.g. a transient error checking whether the
        -- window exists) is never treated as the window being gone: WindowGone
        -- is only emitted on a definitive absence, so a tmux hiccup can never
        -- trigger DetectCrashedAgent.
        -- The split-pinned task is exempt: while the split pane is active
        -- its window has been absorbed into the pane by join-pane, so it
        -- is no longer a standalone window. Both the tick that collects
        -- windows to check and this rule's entry point skip that task, so
        -- pinning never produces a spurious crash (see split-pane.allium).
}

-- == Notifications ==

rule NotifyNeedsInput {
    when: task: core/Task.sub_status transitions_to needs_input

    requires: task.status = running

    ensures:
        if notifications_enabled:
            DesktopNotification(
                task: task,
                urgency: urgent,
                about: needs_input
            )

    @guidance
        -- Notifications are deduplicated: each notification fires at most
        -- once per transition to needs_input. The notified state is
        -- cleared when sub_status leaves needs_input, allowing
        -- re-notification on a subsequent re-entry. The clearing happens
        -- unconditionally, even when notifications are disabled, so that
        -- stale entries do not suppress notifications after re-enabling.
        -- The task is in running/needs_input (the "needs input" section of
        -- the Running column).
        -- needs_input originates in HookNotification's raise path -- it is the
        -- only rule that stamps last_notification_at -- though the writer that
        -- produces the observed transition can be ClassifyAgentActivity, which
        -- re-derives the same state from that stamp on every tick.
        -- Four kinds never take that raise path, and so never originate a
        -- needs_input here: auth_success (ignored outright), the two
        -- elicitation-resolution kinds (which clear instead), and idle_prompt
        -- while the task's own background work is live (see HookNotification).
}

rule NotifyReview {
    when: task: core/Task.status transitions_to review

    ensures:
        if notifications_enabled:
            DesktopNotification(
                task: task,
                urgency: normal,
                about: status_review
            )

    @guidance
        -- Notifications are gated by a user-togglable notifications_enabled
        -- setting (N key in TUI). When disabled, no notifications are sent.
        -- Notifications are deduplicated: each notification fires at most
        -- once per transition to review. The notified state is cleared
        -- when the task leaves review status. The clearing happens
        -- unconditionally, even when notifications are disabled, so that
        -- stale entries do not suppress notifications after re-enabling.
}

-- == Hook-Based Status Transitions ==
-- Claude Code hooks (PreToolUse, Stop, Notification) bridge the agent
-- session lifecycle to task status. These rules describe the CLI hook
-- script `dispatch tui`'s startup configuration check installs (see
-- startup.allium).

rule HookPreToolUse {
    when: PreToolUse(task) or PostToolUse(task)

    requires: task.status = running

    ensures:
        if task.live_subagents = 0 and task.live_shells > 0
           and task.oldest_live_shell_started_at != null
           and now - task.oldest_live_shell_started_at > config.shell_stale_threshold:
            -- A tool call does not vouch for a background shell that has been
            -- running for hours, so the stale_shell signal survives it rather
            -- than being cleared here and re-derived on the next tick.
            task.sub_status = stale_shell
        else:
            task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(running)
    ensures: task.last_pre_tool_use_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Fires on every non-dispatch tool use, for both PreToolUse and
        -- PostToolUse events. Both events are handled identically: they
        -- stamp last_pre_tool_use_at so the task stays Active through the
        -- 10-minute threshold.
        -- The stale_shell branch above is a second copy of the top of
        -- ClassifyAgentActivity's precedence (live subagents beat live shells
        -- beat the time threshold), because the implementation shares one
        -- classifier between the two. It must track that rule's ordering: an
        -- earlier draft dropped the live_subagents conjunct and said
        -- stale_shell where the code says active. Change one, check the other.
        --
        -- PostToolUse is registered in addition to PreToolUse so that the
        -- activity timestamp is refreshed after every tool call completes.
        -- This matters when an agent chains sub-agent invocations: each
        -- sub-agent call fires PreToolUse at the start and PostToolUse
        -- when the call returns, keeping the parent task's timestamp fresh
        -- between invocations even if a single sub-agent runs longer than
        -- ACTIVE_THRESHOLD.
        --
        -- This rule never writes task.status. It is gated to tasks that
        -- are already Running, and is a complete no-op for a task in any
        -- other status — a PreToolUse reaching a Backlog, Review, Done or
        -- Archived task changes nothing at all. Status movement is the
        -- responsibility of other rules: backlog -> running on dispatch
        -- (see dispatch.allium: DispatchTask) and review -> running on
        -- session resume (see HookUserPromptSubmit below).
        -- The sub_status reset to active (default for running) also
        -- clears needs_input when the agent resumes after a notification.
        -- Filtering of dispatch MCP calls (mcp__dispatch__*) happens in
        -- the shell hook script (task-status-hook) before calling
        -- `dispatch hook`. The Rust service layer never sees the tool
        -- name; it only receives the HookEventKind (PreToolUse,
        -- Notification, Stop).
        --
        -- One tool name is inspected for a second purpose, in the same
        -- script and for the same reason the service layer cannot do it:
        -- a PreToolUse for Claude Code's AskUserQuestion tool means the
        -- agent has opened a question dialog and cannot proceed until a
        -- human answers it. That is a block, not activity, so the script
        -- follows the plain activity signal with a
        -- Notification(elicitation_dialog) for the same task, and
        -- HookNotification's Raise path turns it into needs_input. Without
        -- it a blocked agent is indistinguishable from a working one: no
        -- Claude Code Notification hook fires for AskUserQuestion, so the
        -- task would sit in Active with a fresh last_pre_tool_use_at for as
        -- long as the question went unanswered — which is exactly how an
        -- agent waiting on the /wrap-up question stalled unnoticed for
        -- fifty minutes.
        --
        -- Only PreToolUse raises. The answer arrives as this same tool's
        -- PostToolUse, whose ordinary activity stamp puts
        -- last_pre_tool_use_at ahead of last_notification_at again and so
        -- clears the block through the existing classifier — no second
        -- translated event, and no dependence on a matching
        -- elicitation_complete ever being emitted.
}

rule HookStop {
    when: Stop(task)

    requires: task.status = running

    ensures:
        if task.live_subagents > 0 or task.live_shells > 0:
            task.stop_pending = true
            task.stop_pending_at = now
        else:
            task.status = review
            task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(review)
            task.last_pre_tool_use_at = null
            task.last_notification_at = null
            task.stop_pending = false

    @guidance
        -- Stop does not fire inside subagents (Claude Code fires
        -- SubagentStop there instead), so a Stop arriving with live
        -- subagents is legitimate — it means the main agent finished its
        -- turn while background subagents keep working. Flipping to Review
        -- there is what put tasks in Review with work still in flight.
        -- Suppressing it here just defers the same effect: HookSubagentStop
        -- (or one of the other drain points) applies it once the last
        -- subagent drains.
        --
        -- live_shells > 0 defers the same way and for the same reason: a
        -- backgrounded shell (Bash tool with run_in_background = true) keeps
        -- running after the agent's own turn ends, and flipping to Review
        -- here would strand that work invisibly, same as the subagent case.
        -- HookShellStop's drain path (below) applies the deferred Stop once
        -- the last live shell stops.
        --
        -- The real implementation is two separate conditional UPDATE
        -- statements in one transaction (src/db/queries/tasks.rs::
        -- try_record_stop), not a single if/else: a "flip" statement gated
        -- on live_subagents = 0 AND live_shells = 0, and a "defer" statement
        -- gated on live_subagents > 0 OR live_shells > 0. Both must check
        -- both counters — widening only one of the two statements leaves the
        -- other blind to live_shells, silently keeping the exact bug this
        -- rule exists to fix for the case that statement covers.
        --
        -- The branch is evaluated against the task's committed state as part
        -- of the same atomic write, not against a value read beforehand.
        -- Every Claude Code hook runs as its own OS process, so a count read
        -- before the write can already be stale by the time the write lands:
        -- the last subagent may have drained in the interval. Deciding at
        -- write time means whichever process commits second observes the
        -- other's committed value and computes the correct outcome.
        --
        -- The defer branch records when this Stop fired, not when its write
        -- landed: stop_pending_at is the hook's own event time, taken before
        -- the write. HookUserPromptSubmit compares against it, and that
        -- comparison is only sound if the value is independent of write
        -- order — see its guidance.
        --
        -- The flip branch is deliberately conditioned on live_subagents = 0
        -- alone, with no stop_pending precondition: a Stop arriving with no
        -- live subagents flips the task even if stop_pending was already
        -- set. Requiring the bit to be clear would leave a task carrying a
        -- stale bit unable to take either branch.
}

-- == Subagent Counting ==
-- SubagentStart/SubagentStop bracket a subagent's lifetime; SessionStart
-- marks a fresh claude process. Together with session fencing, these keep
-- core/SubagentEntry and task.live_subagents accurate with no TTL — see
-- the "Drift" discussion in the guidance below.

rule HookSubagentStart {
    when: SubagentStart(task, agent_id, session_id)

    ensures:
        let existing = core/SubagentEntry{task, agent_id}
        if exists existing:
            existing.session_id = session_id
        else:
            core/SubagentEntry.created(task: task, agent_id: agent_id, session_id: session_id, started_at: now)
        let live = core/SubagentEntries where task = task
        task.live_subagents = live.count

    @guidance
        -- Idempotent: a replayed start for the same agent_id does not
        -- double-count — the join lookup above finds the existing row and
        -- refreshes its session_id in place instead of creating a second
        -- one. Any entry whose session_id differs from the incoming one is
        -- evicted first — see SubagentSessionFence, which fires on the
        -- same trigger.
}

rule HookSubagentStop {
    when: SubagentStop(task, agent_id, session_id)

    ensures:
        not exists core/SubagentEntry{task, agent_id}
        let live = core/SubagentEntries where task = task
        task.live_subagents = live.count
        if task.live_subagents = 0 and task.live_shells = 0 and task.stop_pending and task.status = running:
            task.status = review
            task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(review)
            task.last_pre_tool_use_at = null
            task.last_notification_at = null
            task.stop_pending = false

    @guidance
        -- An unrecognised agent_id is a no-op, not a count underflow: the
        -- join lookup finds nothing, the removal is trivially satisfied,
        -- and live_subagents is simply recomputed from the table either
        -- way. The conditional block is the "drain path".
        --
        -- Recomputing live_subagents and applying the deferred Stop are ONE
        -- atomic step, not a count write followed by a separate flip. There
        -- is therefore no window in which the count has reached zero but the
        -- flip has not been applied — not even if the hook process dies
        -- partway, since it either commits the whole step or none of it.
        --
        -- This drain condition and HookShellStop's (below) both apply the
        -- SAME deferred Stop through the same underlying predicate (Rust:
        -- apply_pending_stop_if_drained, src/db/queries/subagents.rs, shared
        -- by both). That sharing is load-bearing, not incidental: a
        -- subagent-drain-to-zero must not flip the task while a live shell
        -- is still outstanding, and vice versa. A later edit that widens one
        -- caller's condition without widening the shared predicate — or that
        -- gives shells their own independent copy of this check instead of
        -- routing through the same one — reintroduces exactly the race this
        -- note exists to warn against.
        --
        -- Four structural points clear a task's subagent entries. Exactly
        -- one of them runs this drain path; the other three clear without
        -- draining, and the split is what keeps a task from ever being
        -- simultaneously Crashed-and-in-Review or
        -- freshly-dispatched-and-in-Review. The rule that owns the
        -- resulting status wins:
        --   - DetachTmux (see split-pane.allium) DOES run the drain path.
        --     It assigns no outcome status of its own — a detach just
        --     removes the agent that was going to drain the count itself —
        --     so applying the deferred Stop is safe there, and is exactly
        --     what unsticks a stop_pending task with no agent left to
        --     finish the job.
        --   - DetectCrashedAgent and DispatchTask do NOT run the drain
        --     path. Both already assign the task's resulting status in the
        --     same write (crashed / running respectively), and draining
        --     alongside that write would leave the task in both states at
        --     once — the more informative status wins, so drain is
        --     skipped.
        --   - ClearSubagentsOnSessionStart (below) does NOT run the drain
        --     path either, for a different reason: a new/resumed/cleared
        --     session means the previous turn is over, so the Stop that
        --     turn deferred is stale and gets voided rather than applied.
        --     See its own guidance.
        -- All three non-draining points still clear stop_pending
        -- unconditionally, so the bit cannot survive into a later session
        -- and fire a spurious flip.
        --
        -- ShellEntry mirrors this same split, but with only THREE structural
        -- clear points, not four: DetachTmux (draining, same reasoning as
        -- above), DetectCrashedAgent and DispatchTask (non-draining, same
        -- reasoning as above). There is no ClearShellsOnSessionStart — see
        -- ShellEntry's own doc comment in core.allium and HookShellStart/
        -- HookShellStop below for why a live shell cannot be assumed dead at
        -- a session boundary the way a subagent can.
        --
        -- Those four points are about the SubagentEntry rows. stop_pending
        -- has one further clear point that touches no rows at all: any
        -- status write that moves the task out of running clears it in the
        -- same write — the manual moves and archive (tasks.allium), MCP
        -- update_task (mcp-task-tools.allium) and the session close
        -- (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSession). A deferred Stop belongs to the
        -- turn the task was running under, so letting the bit outlive that
        -- turn would let a later drain flip the task out of a status a human
        -- just chose. See core/Task's PendingStopOnlyWhileRunning invariant,
        -- and ExitSession's guidance for the one thing the close does NOT
        -- clear (the rows and live_subagents).
        --
        -- No backstop rule is needed for the "stranded" state — running AND
        -- stop_pending AND live_subagents = 0. The argument is an invariant
        -- over the writes, not an enumeration of interleavings:
        --   - stop_pending is only ever set to true by a write whose
        --     condition requires live_subagents > 0 (HookStop's defer
        --     branch), so the instant the bit is set the count is non-zero.
        --     The state cannot be created directly.
        --   - live_subagents only reaches zero in a write that applies any
        --     deferred Stop in the same atomic step, so the state cannot be
        --     reached by the count falling either.
        --   - Every other writer of stop_pending —
        --     ClearSubagentsOnSessionStart, HookUserPromptSubmit,
        --     DispatchTask, DetectCrashedAgent, and the status writes
        --     described above — never sets it to true: each either clears
        --     it or leaves it alone (HookUserPromptSubmit is the one that
        --     can leave it, and only when the deferred Stop is newer than
        --     the prompt). Clearing moves away from the state, and leaving
        --     it alone cannot enter one it was not already in.
        -- No write therefore commits into it, regardless of how many hook
        -- processes participate or in what order they commit.
        --
        -- The argument holds going forward, not retroactively: it is an
        -- invariant over the writes as they are now, and an earlier revision
        -- decided the same branches from a prior read rather than at write
        -- time, which could strand a task. Rows written before the invariant
        -- held are resolved once, on upgrade.
}

rule SubagentSessionFence {
    when: SubagentStart(task, _, incoming_session_id) or SubagentStop(task, _, incoming_session_id)

    ensures:
        for entry in core/SubagentEntries where task = task and session_id != incoming_session_id:
            not exists entry

    @guidance
        -- A new claude process means a new session_id, so entries from a
        -- dead session are provably dead. This is the drift bound: there
        -- is deliberately no TTL. Any threshold short enough to reclaim a
        -- phantom promptly is short enough to sweep a legitimately
        -- long-running subagent.
}

rule ClearSubagentsOnSessionStart {
    when: SessionStart(task)

    ensures:
        for entry in core/SubagentEntries where task = task:
            not exists entry
        task.live_subagents = 0
    ensures: task.stop_pending = false

    @guidance
        -- Fires when a fresh claude process starts, a session is resumed,
        -- or the transcript is cleared in the task's tmux window. Clears
        -- unconditionally rather than fencing on a prior session_id — a
        -- SessionStart payload carries no previous session to compare
        -- against, unlike SubagentStart/SubagentStop.
        --
        -- Does NOT run the drain path. A new, resumed or cleared session
        -- means the previous turn is over, so a Stop that turn deferred is
        -- stale: it is voided, not applied. Resume makes this concrete —
        -- RetryResume (dispatch.allium) deliberately keeps the task Running
        -- and continues the existing conversation with no prompt, so
        -- draining here would land the task in Review with a live agent in
        -- its window and no UserPromptSubmit coming to rescue it. This is
        -- the same "the rule that owns the resulting status wins" split as
        -- DetectCrashedAgent and DispatchTask; see the HookSubagentStop
        -- guidance.
        --
        -- Registered on the startup, resume and clear sources only. compact
        -- is excluded because it fires on *auto* compaction too, mid
        -- fan-out: clearing there wipes a genuine live count that the
        -- session fence cannot restore, since the still-running subagents
        -- share this session id and their eventual SubagentStops become
        -- no-op deletes. fork is excluded because a fork is a different
        -- session and must not wipe the original's count.
}

-- == Shell Counting ==
-- Bash (with run_in_background = true) / KillBash|TaskStop / BashOutput|
-- TaskOutput PostToolUse events bracket a backgrounded shell's tracked
-- lifetime. Unlike subagent counting, there is no SessionStart-driven clear
-- -- see ShellSessionFence's guidance and ShellEntry's doc comment in
-- core.allium for why.
--
-- @guidance
--     KillBash/BashOutput and TaskStop/TaskOutput are two tool-name
--     generations for the same stop/poll signal -- the current Claude Code
--     CLI generalised background-task management beyond Bash shells and
--     renamed the tools accordingly. Both are matched because either can be
--     live depending on the CLI version. The id field itself also moved:
--     the hook script resolves `tool_response.backgroundTaskId` (current)
--     before falling back to `tool_response.shell_id` (older) for start, and
--     `tool_input.task_id` (current) before `tool_input.shell_id` (older)
--     for stop/poll -- verified against a live hook payload (#4197), not
--     assumed from docs. The original implementation assumed shell_id/
--     KillBash/BashOutput only, which meant `dispatch hook-shell` was never
--     actually invoked against the current CLI in production.

rule HookShellStart {
    when: PostToolUse(task, tool_name, run_in_background, shell_id, session_id)

    requires: tool_name = "Bash"
    requires: run_in_background = true

    ensures:
        let existing = core/ShellEntry{task, shell_id}
        if exists existing:
            existing.session_id = session_id
        else:
            core/ShellEntry.created(task: task, shell_id: shell_id, session_id: session_id, started_at: now)
        let live = core/ShellEntries where task = task
        task.live_shells = live.count
        task.oldest_live_shell_started_at = (core/ShellEntries where task = task).min(started_at)

    @guidance
        -- Fires on PostToolUse, not PreToolUse: the shell_id Claude Code
        -- assigns to a backgrounded shell does not exist until the Bash call
        -- returns, so there is nothing to key an entry on any earlier.
        -- Idempotent for the same reason HookSubagentStart is: a replayed
        -- start for the same shell_id refreshes session_id in place rather
        -- than double-counting. Any entry whose session_id differs from the
        -- incoming one is evicted first — see ShellSessionFence, which fires
        -- on the same trigger.
}

rule HookShellStop {
    when: PostToolUse(task, tool_name, shell_id, session_id, status?)

    requires: tool_name = "KillBash" or tool_name = "TaskStop"
              or ((tool_name = "BashOutput" or tool_name = "TaskOutput")
                  and status != null and status != "running")

    ensures:
        not exists core/ShellEntry{task, shell_id}
        let live = core/ShellEntries where task = task
        task.live_shells = live.count
        task.oldest_live_shell_started_at = (core/ShellEntries where task = task).min(started_at)
        if task.live_subagents = 0 and task.live_shells = 0 and task.stop_pending and task.status = running:
            task.status = review
            task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(review)
            task.last_pre_tool_use_at = null
            task.last_notification_at = null
            task.stop_pending = false

    @guidance
        -- Two distinct trigger shapes, both definitive stop signals:
        -- KillBash/TaskStop always means the shell is dead; BashOutput/
        -- TaskOutput means it only when the polled status is no longer
        -- "running" (completed/failed/killed) — a poll that still reports
        -- "running" is not a stop event at all and this rule does not fire
        -- for it.
        --
        -- An unrecognised shell_id is a no-op, not a count underflow — same
        -- reasoning as HookSubagentStop. The drain condition here is the
        -- SAME shared predicate HookSubagentStop's drain uses (Rust:
        -- apply_pending_stop_if_drained) — see that rule's guidance for why
        -- routing both through one shared check, rather than two
        -- independent copies, is load-bearing.
        --
        -- This rule has no non-draining counterpart the way
        -- HookSubagentStop does (DetectCrashedAgent / DispatchTask clear
        -- without draining, ClearSubagentsOnSessionStart clears without
        -- draining) — those three non-draining clear points for ShellEntry
        -- are documented on HookSubagentStop's guidance above and on
        -- ShellEntry in core.allium, not as separate rules here, since they
        -- reuse the exact same clear machinery already described there.
}

rule ShellSessionFence {
    when: PostToolUse(task, tool_name, incoming_session_id, run_in_background?, status?)

    requires: (tool_name = "Bash" and run_in_background = true)
              or tool_name = "KillBash" or tool_name = "TaskStop"
              or ((tool_name = "BashOutput" or tool_name = "TaskOutput")
                  and status != null and status != "running")

    ensures:
        for entry in core/ShellEntries where task = task and session_id != incoming_session_id:
            not exists entry

    @guidance
        -- Mirrors SubagentSessionFence exactly, and is deliberately the ONLY
        -- session-boundary sweep for ShellEntry — there is no
        -- ClearShellsOnSessionStart. A subagent cannot survive a session
        -- boundary at all, so unconditionally clearing on every SessionStart
        -- is safe for SubagentEntry. A backgrounded shell is an independent
        -- OS process and CAN survive a `/clear` or `resume` — unconditionally
        -- clearing ShellEntry on SessionStart would silently drop tracking of
        -- a shell that outlived the boundary, reproducing this design's
        -- headline bug via the session boundary instead of via a bare Stop.
        --
        -- So ShellEntry relies on fencing alone: the moment ANY new shell
        -- event (start, kill, or a poll) arrives carrying a new session_id,
        -- every row from the old session is swept, because a new session_id
        -- proves the old rows can no longer be validly referenced by this
        -- session. If no new shell event ever arrives in a later session for
        -- a task that had live shells before the boundary, those rows are
        -- NOT swept here — they persist until one of the three real
        -- structural clear points (DetectCrashedAgent, DetachTmux,
        -- DispatchTask) reaches them, or indefinitely if none does. This is
        -- the accepted "Known limitation" tradeoff — see
        -- docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-15-shell-visibility-design.md.
        -- config.shell_stale_threshold (ClassifyAgentActivity) is what keeps
        -- that indefinite case from rendering identically to a healthy
        -- long-running shell forever.
}

rule HookUserPromptSubmit {
    when: UserPromptSubmit(task)

    requires: task.status in {running, review}

    ensures: task.status = running
    ensures: task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(running)
    ensures: task.last_pre_tool_use_at = now
    ensures:
        if task.stop_pending_at = null or task.stop_pending_at < now:
            task.stop_pending = false

    @guidance
        -- A human resuming the conversation voids the Stop deferred by the
        -- turn that just ended: that turn is over, and this rule now owns
        -- the resulting status (running). Leaving the bit set would let a
        -- later drain point flip the task to Review underneath an active
        -- turn.
        --
        -- The clear is conditional because "the turn that just ended" is
        -- not the only Stop the bit can hold. Every Claude Code hook is a
        -- separate OS process, so this rule's write can land *after* a Stop
        -- belonging to the turn this very prompt started: the prompt fires
        -- at t0, the agent's turn ends at t1 with subagents still live, the
        -- Stop defers at t2, and this rule's write lands at t3. Voiding
        -- there deletes a Stop that no drain will ever re-create —
        -- HookSubagentStop's drain path requires the bit — so the task
        -- stays running with no agent left to move it. Requiring the
        -- deferred Stop to have fired strictly before this prompt
        -- distinguishes the two: t_stop < now is last turn's Stop and is
        -- voided, t_stop > now is this turn's and survives to be drained.
        --
        -- The comparison is against event times, not write times, and that
        -- is what makes it sound. A generation counter — incremented here,
        -- stamped by HookStop's defer — cannot work: at t2 the Stop reads
        -- the counter before this rule's t3 write increments it, so its
        -- recorded generation looks stale and the clear voids it anyway.
        -- Any ordering derived from write order inherits the race the rule
        -- is trying to resolve.
        --
        -- Ties are reachable, which is why they need a rule: the two events
        -- are recorded at a finer resolution than the other hook timestamps
        -- precisely because a Stop and the prompt that supersedes it can
        -- fall inside the same second, and any resolution still admits a
        -- pair too close to separate.
        --
        -- Ties (equal timestamps) preserve the bit. That is the safer
        -- direction: a bit wrongly kept is resolved by the next drain or
        -- the next prompt, whereas one wrongly voided strands the task in
        -- running permanently. A null stop_pending_at is the one exception
        -- — it means the deferred Stop predates the field, so it reads as
        -- "fired before any prompt" and is voided, preserving the
        -- unconditional behaviour those rows were written under.
        --
        -- Fires when the user submits a new prompt in the Claude Code
        -- session, before the agent has taken any action. It is the
        -- earliest available signal that a human has resumed the
        -- conversation, so a Review task flips back to Running the moment
        -- the message is sent — without waiting for the agent's first
        -- PreToolUse hook, which may be seconds away, or may never fire at
        -- all if the agent's turn is pure text with no tool call.
        --
        -- Unlike HookPreToolUse, this rule is intentionally not gated to
        -- already-Running tasks: Review -> Running is exactly the
        -- transition it exists to drive. It is still a no-op outside
        -- {running, review} (e.g. a stale UserPromptSubmit reaching a
        -- worktree whose task has since moved to done/archived).
        --
        -- Epic status is recalculated only when this rule actually changes
        -- task.status (Review -> Running), mirroring HookStop. Firing while
        -- already Running is treated as a plain activity refresh and does
        -- not trigger a recalculation.
}

rule HookNotification {
    when: Notification(task, kind?)
    -- Like HookPreToolUse's, this `requires` is a silent no-op rather than a
    -- rejection: a notification for a task that has left running describes a
    -- state that has since moved on, and the hook process has no caller to
    -- report to. The predicate rides in the write itself (see below), so a
    -- task that leaves running concurrently simply matches no row.
    requires: task.status = running

    ensures:
        if (kind = null or kind in {permission_prompt, elicitation_dialog})
            or (kind = idle_prompt and task.live_shells = 0 and task.live_subagents = 0):
            -- Raise: agent is genuinely blocked (or the payload predates the
            -- notification_type field, in which case we preserve the historical
            -- "always needs_input" behaviour as a safe default).
            task.sub_status = needs_input
            task.last_notification_at = now
        else if kind in {elicitation_complete, elicitation_response}:
            -- Clear: an elicitation just resolved, so the block is over. Mirror
            -- the HookPreToolUse resume path — return to active and drop the
            -- notification timestamp — so the card flips back the instant the
            -- user answers, without waiting for the next PreToolUse.
            task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(running)
            task.last_notification_at = null
        -- Ignore: no branch matches, so sub_status and last_notification_at are
        -- left untouched (no-op). Two cases land here: kind = auth_success,
        -- which is informational only, and an idle_prompt raised while the
        -- task's own background work is still live — see the guidance below.

    @guidance
        -- HookNotification fires when Claude Code raises a notification. The
        -- shell hook (task-status-hook) reads the payload's `notification_type`
        -- and forwards it as an optional `--kind` argument:
        --   dispatch hook <id> notification [--kind <notification_type>]
        -- `NotificationWrite::from_kind` (models/tasks.rs) resolves the kind
        -- alone into the write to apply; record_hook_event hands that straight
        -- to `record_notification` (db/queries/tasks.rs), which applies it as
        -- one conditional statement. See the branches above. `kind` is
        -- core/NotificationKind.
        --
        -- Evaluation time. The idle_prompt branch is the only one that reads
        -- task state, and it is evaluated against the task's committed state as
        -- part of the same atomic write, not against a value read beforehand --
        -- the identical requirement HookStop states for the identical two
        -- counters, and for the identical reason: every Claude Code hook runs
        -- as its own OS process, so a count read before the write can already
        -- be stale by the time the write lands. `NotificationWrite` is resolved
        -- from the kind alone precisely so the live-work predicate can travel
        -- into the statement rather than being settled in front of it. A row
        -- the predicate rejects is a silent no-op.
        --
        -- HookPreToolUse reads the same two counters from a plain snapshot and
        -- does not need this treatment: it re-derives a sub_status that
        -- ClassifyAgentActivity recomputes from fresh state on the very next
        -- tick, so a stale read there self-corrects within a tick. A stamped
        -- last_notification_at does not: it outlives the tick that read it,
        -- and only a later write ends the pin -- a PreToolUse or
        -- UserPromptSubmit stamping a newer last_pre_tool_use_at, a clear-kind
        -- notification, or the move to review nulling both. That asymmetry is
        -- what makes the atomic write load-bearing here and merely tidy there.
        --
        -- Classification (core/NotificationKind):
        --   raise  -> permission_prompt, elicitation_dialog, and
        --             absent/unknown (compat): (running, needs_input),
        --             the "needs input" section of the Running column; stamps
        --             last_notification_at.
        --             idle_prompt raises too, but only when the task has no
        --             live background work of its own -- see below.
        --   clear  -> elicitation_complete, elicitation_response: back to
        --             (running, active), last_notification_at = null.
        --   ignore -> auth_success: no state change, no timestamp write, so an
        --             auth-success toast no longer raises a false needs_input.
        --             idle_prompt while live_shells > 0 or live_subagents > 0
        --             lands here for the same reason -- see below.
        --
        -- Why idle_prompt is conditional. An agent that backgrounds a shell
        -- (Bash with run_in_background) ends its turn while that shell keeps
        -- running: HookStop defers the flip to review because live_shells > 0,
        -- and Claude Code, seeing a session that has stopped producing output,
        -- fires Notification(idle_prompt) about a minute later. Nothing is
        -- waiting on a human -- the agent is waiting on its own work -- so
        -- raising needs_input there reports a block that does not exist.
        -- Observed on tasks #4608 and #4478, both of which sat in the Blocked
        -- column for hours with stop_pending = 1 and live_shells > 0 while
        -- their agents waited on `sbt reference/test`.
        --
        -- Not stamping last_notification_at is the load-bearing half of the
        -- suppression, not an incidental one. ClassifyAgentActivity reads only
        -- timestamps, so a stamped last_notification_at newer than
        -- last_pre_tool_use_at re-pins needs_input on every tick and the card
        -- cannot recover until the next PreToolUse. Leaving it null lets that
        -- rule fall through to its live_subagents / live_shells branches, which
        -- read active (or stale_shell past shell_stale_threshold).
        --
        -- Suppression cannot strand a task in Blocked, because it writes
        -- nothing to be stranded by: the task keeps whatever
        -- ClassifyAgentActivity derives from its live counts and timestamps
        -- (active, then stale or stale_shell). Nor does it strand one in
        -- running: when the last shell or subagent drains, any Stop deferred
        -- behind it applies and the task moves to review (HookShellStop /
        -- HookSubagentStop). An idle_prompt that arrived with no Stop deferred
        -- behind it leaves the task running, which is correct -- the agent's
        -- turn never ended.
        --
        -- permission_prompt and elicitation_dialog stay unconditional, live
        -- background work or not: a permission decision or a question dialog
        -- genuinely needs a human while shells churn. This mirrors
        -- ClassifyAgentActivity's ordering, where needs_input outranks
        -- live_subagents for exactly that reason. An absent kind is
        -- unconditional too -- it may be a permission prompt from an older
        -- Claude Code, and a false Blocked is cheaper than a missed real one.
        --
        -- Only the raise path triggers NotifyNeedsInput (desktop notification),
        -- because that fires on the transition into needs_input. When the agent
        -- resumes after a raise, HookPreToolUse resets sub_status to active.
        --
        -- Not every Notification(elicitation_dialog) originates in a Claude
        -- Code Notification hook. The shell hook also synthesises one from a
        -- PreToolUse for the AskUserQuestion tool, which raises no
        -- notification of its own — see HookPreToolUse's guidance. This rule
        -- treats both identically on purpose: an agent blocked on a question
        -- it asked is in the same state as one blocked on a dialog Claude
        -- Code announced, and the raise path is what makes that visible.
        --
        -- Backward compatibility: `--kind` is optional. Hook scripts (or older
        -- Claude Code payloads) that omit it fall into the raise branch via the
        -- `kind = null` guard, preserving the pre-existing behaviour exactly.
        --
        -- CLI surface note: `dispatch hook <id> notification [--kind K]` is a
        -- non-MCP, hook-driven entry point implemented by cmd_hook. Unlike the
        -- MCP update_task tool — which blocks transitions into `done`/`archived`
        -- so agents cannot self-promote past Review — the hook CLI drives the
        -- lifecycle steps that agents must not perform on their own.
}

rule HookPeerMessageSent {
    -- Fires when a task's agent calls Claude Code's native `SendMessage`
    -- tool (task #4098). Dispatch never performs this delivery itself —
    -- agents message each other directly, addressed by the `task-<id>`
    -- session name dispatch assigns at launch — this rule only observes the
    -- call, via the same Claude Code hook pipeline as HookPreToolUse /
    -- HookNotification, to drive the TUI flash and reconstruct an audit
    -- trail. See mcp-task-tools.allium's note on why `send_message` (the
    -- former MCP tool) was replaced by this.
    -- SendMessageObserved is PostToolUse already filtered to tool = "SendMessage"
    -- — a named, pre-filtered trigger rather than a raw PostToolUse annotated
    -- with a literal tool name (which isn't valid trigger syntax for a
    -- listening rule; literal annotations are only for trigger *emissions*).
    -- It is now the only trigger of that shape: agent-tree.allium had one, but
    -- retired it when its pane stopped reading tool calls and started asking
    -- git instead.
    when: SendMessageObserved(sender, to, message)

    let target = core/Task.find_by_session_name(to)

    ensures: sender.last_peer_message_sent_at = now
    ensures:
        if target != null:
            target.last_peer_message_received_at = now

    @guidance
        -- `tool_input.to` is Claude Code's own field name for the message's
        -- addressee (not `name` — verified against the live tool schema,
        -- since the docs describing this feature don't spell out the
        -- payload shape). It may carry a disambiguating `" [ref]"` suffix
        -- per `ListAgents`' listing format; `parse_peer_message_target_name`
        -- (src/service/tasks/crud.rs — the `core/Task.find_by_session_name`
        -- of the rule body above) strips it before delegating to
        -- `TmuxWindow::task_id` (src/models/tmux_window.rs), the same
        -- parser tmux window names go through, so the two conventions
        -- cannot drift apart.
        -- A `to` that doesn't parse to that shape, or a task id that no
        -- longer exists, is not an error — the sender addressed a session
        -- outside dispatch's own fleet, or one since deleted — only
        -- `sender.last_peer_message_sent_at` is stamped in that case.
        --
        -- Implemented by `dispatch hook-peer-message <sender_id> --target
        -- <to> --body <message>` (src/main.rs, cmd_hook_peer_message),
        -- invoked from `plugin/hooks/scripts/task-status-hook`'s
        -- `PostToolUse` case — one of that case's additive observers,
        -- alongside the backgrounded-shell forwarding, and gated on
        -- `tool_name == "SendMessage"`
        -- rather than the `mcp__dispatch__*` skip (a native tool call is
        -- never skipped by that filter). `TaskService::record_peer_message_sent`
        -- (src/service/tasks/crud.rs) does the two stamps and also appends a
        -- `SendMessage` trajectory entry for the sender
        -- (`src/mcp/trajectory.rs::append_entry`, called directly from the
        -- CLI process) — the only audit record this call gets, since it
        -- never reaches dispatch's own MCP server.
        --
        -- Both timestamps are read by the TUI's tick-driven task diff
        -- (`detect_task_transition_notifications`, src/tui/update/agent.rs)
        -- the same way `last_notification_at` already drives `needs_input`
        -- detection: a changed value inserts into the corresponding
        -- sent/received flash map. See board-visuals.allium's "Message flash" for the
        -- rendering contract.
        --
        -- Only `PostToolUse` fires this — `PreToolUse` for the same call is
        -- ignored, since the message hasn't actually been sent until the
        -- tool call completes.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Q1: Should stale/crashed detection trigger automatic status changes
--     (e.g., moving back to Backlog), or should it remain advisory-only
--     as it is today?
